co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary Meeting","webcast":true,"url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"The Blueprint efficiency programme was scaled back due to Local Government Reorganisation uncertainty, now targeting \u00a3900,000 of savings in 2026/27 to support financial sustainability until reorganisation.","lead":"The council's projected medium-term deficits depend on delivery of Blueprint savings; scaling back the efficiency programme due to reorganisation uncertainty weakens the plan to close the funding gap.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Housing stock management was emergency insourced in December 2025 after replacement contractor Axis Europe was terminated after six months due to performance failures.","lead":"Outsourced service failure followed by emergency insourcing creates operational delivery risk, financial exposure from disputed claims, and demonstrates the council lost confidence in external delivery model.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"The council has \u00a3307m of borrowing underpinning a \u00a3366m capital financing requirement, primarily to fund commercial property investments designed to generate revenue income.","lead":"Large-scale commercial borrowing and a revenue budget dependent on commercial property income creates exposure to property market volatility and income collection risk, particularly material given projected revenue deficits.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","headline":"The Blueprint efficiency programme was scaled back due to Local Government Reorganisation uncertainty, now targeting \u00a3900,000 of savings in 2026/27 to support financial sustainability until reorganisation.","detail":"Blueprint Uttlesford was scaled back in early 2025 with wider work paused in April due to uncertainty from Local Government Reorganisation. A narrower Blueprint 2.0 programme is now underway, focused on delivering a Workforce Plan, achieving financial savings to support a sustainable position until reorganisation, and completing major cross-cutting projects including office decarbonisation and implementation of a new Technology 1 finance system. The programme is targeting \u00a32.5m of savings by 2030/31, including \u00a3900,000 in 2026/27.","category":"savings","matters":"The council's projected medium-term deficits depend on delivery of Blueprint savings; scaling back the efficiency programme due to reorganisation uncertainty weakens the plan to close the funding gap.","figures":"\u00a32.5m savings target by 2030/31; \u00a3900,000 in 2026/27; programme scaled back April 2025","quote":"Blueprint Uttlesford was scaled back in early 2025 due to Local Government Reorganisation, with wider work paused in April. ... The programme has an agreed scope and governance structure and is targeting \u00a32.5 million in savings by 2030/31, including \u00a3900,000 in 2026/27.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","headline":"Housing stock management was emergency insourced in December 2025 after replacement contractor Axis Europe was terminated after six months due to performance failures.","detail":"Uttlesford Norse Services Ltd's housing management contract ended 31 March 2025 and was replaced by Axis Europe from 1 April 2025. Axis Europe's contract was terminated after only six months due to several performance issues. In December 2025, Cabinet elected to bring the service in-house. A procurement exercise was completed to establish contracts for in-house operation. Contractual disputes with both the previous contractor (UNSL) and Axis Europe remain ongoing.","category":"governance","matters":"Outsourced service failure followed by emergency insourcing creates operational delivery risk, financial exposure from disputed claims, and demonstrates the council lost confidence in external delivery model.","figures":"Contract ended 31 March 2025; replacement began 1 April 2025; terminated after six months; insourced December 2025","quote":"The Axis Europe contract was subsequently terminated six months after commencement because of several performance issues. In December, Cabinet considered options for bringing the service in-house, after which the service was insourced.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","headline":"The council has \u00a3307m of borrowing underpinning a \u00a3366m capital financing requirement, primarily to fund commercial property investments designed to generate revenue income.","detail":"Uttlesford has a capital financing requirement of \u00a3366m as of 31 March 2026, reflecting significant commercial property investments across England. The investment portfolio consists of seven properties, including Chesterford Research Park held through joint venture subsidiary Aspire (CRP) Limited. A commercial strategy uses investment income to support the underlying cost base. The financing is supported by \u00a3198m of long-term borrowings and \u00a3109m of short-term borrowings.","category":"capital","matters":"Large-scale commercial borrowing and a revenue budget dependent on commercial property income creates exposure to property market volatility and income collection risk, particularly material given projected revenue deficits.","figures":"\u00a3366m capital financing requirement, \u00a3198m long-term borrowings, \u00a3109m short-term borrowings, seven properties in portfolio","quote":"The Council had a capital financing requirement of \u00a3366m on 31 March 2026 (see note 38 to the draft financial statements), reflecting the scale of investments that have been made.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"UDC VFM Risk assessment 25-26 - Final draft","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45186/45186.pdf","headline":"The council is moving from reserve drawdown avoidance in 2025/26 to projected deficits requiring use of MTFS Reserve from 2026/27 onwards due to reduced government funding.","detail":"Uttlesford achieved an underspend of \u00a30.854m in 2025/26 without drawing on reserves, performing better than the original plan which assumed reserve use. However, the Medium-Term Financial Plan (2026/27 to 2029/30) projects deficits throughout its period due to significant reductions in government funding following the Fair Funding Review, requiring use of the MTFS Reserve from 2026/27 onwards. The council holds sufficient reserves to cover these deficits.","category":"reserves","matters":"A shift to reserve-funded deficits indicates the council's structural finances are under pressure and dependent on accumulated reserves for medium-term sustainability.","figures":"\u00a30.854m underspend 2025/26; MTFS Reserve to be used from 2026/27; deficits projected throughout five-year period to 2029/30","quote":"However, based on the February 2026 Budget and Medium-Term Financial Plan (MTFP), the Council expects to use the MTFS Reserve from 2026/27 onwards. This reflects the significant reduction in government funding following the Fair Funding Review.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Audit and Standards Committee - 24 September 2026_","paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45182/45182.pdf","headline":"Total overdue audit recommendations increased to 33, including 1 critical and 9 high-priority items.","detail":"As of 31 August 2026, the council had 53 outstanding audit recommendations (1 critical, 19 high-priority), of which 33 were overdue (1 critical, 9 high-priority). This represents an increase in high-priority overdue items since 1 April 2026, when 7 of 10 high-priority recommendations were overdue. Notable areas with long-standing unresolved recommendations include debt recovery (from 2024), temporary accommodation procurement (from 2024), and housing project management.","category":"audit","matters":"An accumulation of overdue critical and high-priority audit recommendations indicates the council is not addressing identified control weaknesses in a timely manner, which could allow problems to persist or compound.","figures":"53 outstanding (1 critical, 19 high-priority); 33 overdue (1 critical, 9 high-priority) as of 31 August 2026","quote":"On 31 August 2026, there were 53 recommendations outstanding (including one critical and 19 high priority), of which 33 were past their original due date (including 1 critical and 9 high priority).","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-10"},"name":"Uttlesford","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Planning Development Appeals generating \u00a3124k unexpected overspend","lead":"Specific budget pressure on planning function suggests either increased appeals volume or cost per appeal rising. Indicates dispute resolution costs or increased specialist expertise required.","also":["42 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Standards Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":43,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Quarter 1 Finance Monitor report","top_paper_url":"https://uttlesford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45022/45022.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":113,"news_drafts_total":3}}